  <section*|Introduction>

  We start from stochastic quantisation (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:sq>):
  the GFF is the stationary marginal of a Langevin dynamics in an auxiliary
  time, and the one-point marginal of an elliptic equation in two additional
  dimensions. This is a first way to describe a Euclidean field by an
  equation for the field itself.

  We then change the point of view (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:obs>):
  instead of a single random distribution, we look at the field observed at
  every finite resolution, and take as primary objects the stochastic
  observables of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>: martingales in the filtration of
  finite-resolution measurements.

  Sections, equations and exercises keep the numbering of the whole course:
  references to Sections 1\U3 are to the notes of Lectures 1\U2.

  <paragraph|Structure.> Section<nbsp><reference|sec:sq> treats the
  stochastic quantisation of the GFF. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:obs>
  introduces covariance profiles, the field at resolution <math|t> and
  stochastic observables, and Section<nbsp><reference|sec:gff-obs> studies
  the observables of the GFF and their products. Interactions, the Polchinski
  equation and the forward\Ubackward equations are the subject of Lecture 4.

  <paragraph|Conventions.> As in Lectures 1\U2:
  <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
  <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>>, <math|C=A<rsup|-1>> with kernel <math|G>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> the GFF, <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,g|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>f*g>
  for real functions. For a functional <math|F> on smooth fields,
  <math|D*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
  and <math|D<rsup|2>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> are its first and second
  derivatives, <math|D<rsub|h>*F=<around|\<langle\>|D*F,h|\<rangle\>>>.
  <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>> are the Hermite polynomials of variance
  <math|c>, and <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q>> is the centred Gaussian measure with
  covariance <math|Q>.

  <assign|section-nr|3><section|Stochastic quantisation><label|sec:sq>

  Stochastic quantisation describes a Euclidean field as the marginal of a
  stochastic dynamics. For the GFF the dynamics are linear and everything can
  be computed explicitly; we give two versions, parabolic and elliptic.

  <subsection|The GFF as stationary marginal of parabolic stochastic
  quantisation>

  <paragraph|Langevin dynamics.> If <math|S:\<bbb-R\><rsup|N>\<to\>\<bbb-R\>>
  is smooth with <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<in\>L<rsup|1>>, the diffusion
  <math|\<mathd\>X<rsub|t>=-\<nabla\>*S<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|t>>
  has generator <math|\<cal-L\>=\<Delta\>-\<nabla\>*S\<cdot\>\<nabla\>>, and
  <math|\<cal-L\><rsup|\<ast\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>=\<nabla\>\<cdot\><around|(|\<nabla\>\<mathe\><rsup|-S>+\<mathe\><rsup|-S>\<nabla\>*S|)>=0>:
  the Gibbs measure <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-S<around|(|x|)>>\<mathd\>x/Z> is
  invariant. Parisi and Wu<nbsp><cite|PW81> proposed to apply this to the
  Euclidean action, introducing a fictitious \Pstochastic time\Q <math|t>:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:SQE>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\>=-<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)>*\<Phi\>-V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Phi\>|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<space|2em><text|on
    >\<bbb-R\><rsub|+>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
  </equation>

  with <math|\<xi\>> space-time white noise. This is <em|(parabolic)
  stochastic quantisation>. For <math|V=0> it is the stochastic heat
  equation, an infinite-dimensional Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process.

  <paragraph|History.> Parisi and Wu<nbsp><cite|PW81> introduced stochastic
  quantisation to do perturbation theory for gauge theories without gauge
  fixing. The physics literature of the 1980s is reviewed by Damgaard and
  Hüffel<nbsp><cite|DH87>, whose reprint volume<nbsp><cite|DH88> collects the
  original papers. Jona-Lasinio and Mitter<nbsp><cite|JLM85> gave the first
  mathematical construction of the dynamics for
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> on the torus, where the
  Wick-ordered interaction makes <eqref|eq:SQE> a singular SPDE. They used
  Girsanov's theorem for a variant of <eqref|eq:SQE> in which the drift is
  smoothed by <math|<around|(|1-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>> and the
  noise has covariance <math|<around|(|1-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-\<varepsilon\>>>
  (a slightly coloured noise), which still has the
  <math|P<around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsub|2>> measure as invariant measure. A
  recent review of the mathematical theory is<nbsp><cite|Gub25>.

  Space-time white noise on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> is a centred
  Gaussian family <math|<around|(|\<xi\><around|(|u|)>|)><rsub|u\<in\>L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>>
  with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<xi\><around|(|u|)>*\<xi\><around|(|v|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>,
  formally <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<xi\>*<around|(|t,x|)>*\<xi\>*<around|(|s,y|)>|]>=\<delta\>*<around|(|t-s|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>.
  Let <math|W<rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<xi\>*<around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|[|0,t|]>>\<otimes\>h|)>>,
  <math|t\<geq\>0>, a cylindrical Brownian motion. For a (weak) solution
  <math|\<Phi\>> of <eqref|eq:SQE> with <math|V=0> and
  <math|F=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>,\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|n>|)>|)>\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>,
  Itô's formula shows that <math|F<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>-<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>\<cal-L\>*F<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|r>|)>\<mathd\>r>
  is a martingale, with

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:generator>\<cal-L\>*F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>-<big|sum><rsub|i>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g<around|(|\<ldots\>|)>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|DS <math|\<Rightarrow\>> invariance><label|prop:inf-inv> For
    <math|F,G\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>:
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|G*<space|0.17em>\<cal-L\>*F|]>=-\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>><around|[|<around|\<langle\>|D*G,D*F|\<rangle\>><rsub|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>|]>>.
    In particular <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\><rsub|m>>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*F|]>=0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    We may assume <math|F=g*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>>
    and <math|G=k*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|f<rsub|1>|)>,\<ldots\>|)>>
    with the same <math|f<rsub|i>>. Apply
    <hlink|(3.10)|cqft_notes_L2.tm#eq:DS> of Lecture 2 to the cylinder
    function <math|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g> with
    <math|h=f<rsub|i>> and sum over <math|i>:
    <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g*<space|0.17em>\<varphi\>*<around|(|A*f<rsub|i>|)>|]>=<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|\<langle\>|f<rsub|i>,f<rsub|j>|\<rangle\>>*\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<partial\><rsub|j>*k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i>*g+k*<space|0.17em>\<partial\><rsub|i*j>*g|]>>.
    Insert this into <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|G*\<cal-L\>*F|]>>.
  </proof>

  So the static characterisation (DS) and the dynamic one (invariance under
  the Langevin flow) are two faces of the same integration by parts formula.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Parabolic stochastic quantisation of the GFF><label|thm:parabolic>
    Let <math|\<xi\>> be space-time white noise on
    <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>. For <math|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>> and
    <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> define

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:stat-sol>\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>\<assign\><sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around|(|K<rsub|t,f>|)>,<space|2em>K<rsub|t,f><around|(|r,x|)>\<assign\><with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|{|r\<less\>t|}>><around*|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>f|)><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation>

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item><math|\<Phi\>> is a centred Gaussian process with
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>*\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A>C*g|\<rangle\>>>.
      In particular <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>>
      is stationary and, for every <math|t>, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> has a
      version with values in <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
      whose law is <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>.

      <item><math|\<Phi\>> is a mild and weak solution of <eqref|eq:SQE> with
      <math|V=0>: for <math|s\<less\>t> and
      <math|h\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,

      <\gather*>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-s|)>*A>h|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>h|)>,>>|<row|<cell|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|h|)>=\<Phi\><rsub|s><around|(|h|)>-<big|int><rsub|s><rsup|t>\<Phi\><rsub|r>*<around|(|A*h|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>r+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\>*<around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>>\<otimes\>h|)>.>>>>
      </gather*>

      <item>Let <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>> be an
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued random variable
      independent of <math|\<xi\>>, and let <math|\<Psi\>> be the mild
      solution started from <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>>,

      <\equation*>
        \<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>=\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>+<sqrt|2>*<space|0.17em>\<xi\><around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|0,t|)>><around|(|r|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>f|)>,<space|2em>t\<geq\>0.
      </equation*>

      Then <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>|]>\<to\>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
      as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> for every
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>. Moreover
      <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> is invariant (<math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>>
      implies <math|\<Psi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>> for all <math|t>),
      and it is the unique invariant probability measure of the dynamics.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (a) Since <math|A> is self-adjoint and commutes with
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-u*A>>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|t,f>,K<rsub|s,g>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|-\<infty\>><rsup|t\<wedge\>s><around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t+s-2*r|)>*A>g|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>r=<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|\||t-s|\|>*A><around|(|2*A|)><rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|K<rsub|t,f>\<in\>L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>,
    and multiplying by <math|2> gives the covariance. Stationarity follows.
    For fixed <math|t>, <math|f\<mapsto\>\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>> is
    linear and <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)><rsup|2>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>\<leq\>m<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>><rsup|2>>,
    so it is continuous from <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> to
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-P\>|)>>. By the regularisation theorem for
    random linear functionals on nuclear spaces (Itô\UNawata, Mitoma) it has
    an <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued version, which
    is centred Gaussian with covariance <math|C>, i.e. has law
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>. On the torus one can see this directly: the
    coordinates <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t><around|(|e<rsub|n>|)>> are independent
    stationary OU processes <math|\<mathd\>X=-\<lambda\><rsub|n>*X\<mathd\>t+<sqrt|2>\<mathd\>B>
    with variance <math|\<lambda\><rsub|n><rsup|-1>>, and
    <hlink|(3.3)|cqft_notes_L2.tm#eq:series> of Lecture 2 applies.

    (b) The mild formula is the identity <math|K<rsub|t,h>=K<rsub|s,\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-s|)>*A>h>+<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|(|s,t|)>><around|(|r|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|t-r|)>*A>h>
    together with the linearity of <math|\<xi\>>. The weak formulation
    follows from the mild one by a stochastic Fubini argument
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:weak>).

    (c) Since <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>> is independent of <math|\<xi\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|t><around|(|f|)>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>>|]><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-2*t*A>|)>*C*f|\<rangle\>>>.
    </equation*>

    For <math|m\<gtr\>0>, <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f\<to\>0> in
    <math|\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, so
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|-t*A>f|)>\<to\>0> a.s.
    because <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>.
    By dominated convergence the first factor tends to <math|1>, while the
    second tends to <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>.
    If <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsub|m>>, the first factor equals
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,\<mathe\><rsup|-2*t*A>C*f|\<rangle\>>>>
    and the product is exactly <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|f,C*f|\<rangle\>>>>,
    which proves invariance. If <math|\<nu\>> is invariant, start from
    <math|\<Psi\><rsub|0>\<sim\>\<nu\>>: then
    <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Psi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<nu\>> for all <math|t>,
    so the characteristic functional of <math|\<nu\>> equals that of
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, and <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|she_relaxation.pdf>|0.95par|||><label|fig:she>>|Parabolic
  stochastic quantisation of the GFF on a circle of length <math|8>,
  <math|m=1>, started from <math|\<Phi\><rsub|0>=0>. Left: a sample of the
  solution. Right: each Fourier mode is an Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process, whose
  variance relaxes to <math|\<lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>> at rate
  <math|2*\<lambda\><rsub|k>> (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(c)).
  Dots: Monte Carlo estimates with <math|4000> samples.>

  <\remark>
    As a Gaussian field on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>, the stationary
    solution has covariance <math|2*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t>+A|)><rsup|-1>=2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
    and its equal-time slice has covariance
    <math|2*<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\>><around|(|\<theta\><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>*<frac|\<mathd\>\<theta\>|2*\<pi\>>=A<rsup|-1>>.
    The stochastic time <math|t> is unrelated to the Euclidean time
    <math|\<tau\>>. For interacting theories, <eqref|eq:SQE> is a singular
    SPDE. It was solved by Da Prato and Debussche for
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>><nbsp><cite|DPD03> (see
    also<nbsp><cite|JLM85|AR91>), and for <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>
    using regularity structures and paracontrolled
    calculus<nbsp><cite|Hai14|GIP15|CC18>. Global-in-time
    bounds<nbsp><cite|MW17|GH19> then allow constructing the Euclidean
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> theory as the invariant measure of the
    dynamics<nbsp><cite|GH21>.
  </remark>

  <subsection|The GFF as one-point marginal of elliptic stochastic
  quantisation>

  Parisi and Sourlas<nbsp><cite|PS79> discovered that the elliptic SPDE in
  <math|d+2> dimensions

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:ESQ><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>|)>*\<Psi\>+V<rprime|'><around|(|\<Psi\>|)>=\<zeta\>,<space|2em>\<zeta\>*<text|white
    noise on >\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>,
  </equation>

  is related to the <math|d>-dimensional Gibbs measure: formally, the law of
  <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,\<cdot\>|)>>, <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>, is
  <math|\<propto\>exp <around*|(|-4*\<pi\>*<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,A*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<big|int>V<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>|]>|)>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>
  (<em|dimensional reduction>). The underlying mechanism is a hidden
  supersymmetry<nbsp><cite|PS79|KLP84>. In<nbsp><cite|KLP84> the dimensional
  reduction of the supersymmetric representation is proved rigorously, but
  not the supersymmetric representation of the solution of the SPDE; the full
  nonlinear statement was proved for <math|d=0> in<nbsp><cite|ADG20> and for
  some two-dimensional models (polynomial interactions on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, exponential interactions on
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>) in<nbsp><cite|ADG21>; elliptic
  <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> equations in <math|4> and <math|5> dimensions are
  solved in<nbsp><cite|GH19>. For <math|V=0> the statement is elementary, and
  it explains the factor <math|4*\<pi\>>. We normalise the noise so that the
  slice is exactly <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Elliptic stochastic quantisation of the GFF><label|thm:elliptic>
    Let <math|\<zeta\>> be white noise on
    <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> (an
    <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    Gaussian variable with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<zeta\><around|(|u|)>*\<zeta\><around|(|v|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|u,v|\<rangle\>>>)
    and <math|<math-ss|L>\<assign\>m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>=-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>+A>.

    <\enumerate-alpha>
      <item>The equation <math|<math-ss|L>\<Psi\>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\>>
      has a unique <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
      solution, <math|\<Psi\><around|(|u|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1>u|)>>.

      <item>For <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> and
      <math|f\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> the function
      <math|K<rsub|z,f>\<assign\><math-ss|L><rsup|-1><around|(|\<delta\><rsub|z>\<otimes\>f|)>>
      lies in <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>,
      and <math|z\<mapsto\>K<rsub|z,f>> is continuous. The Wiener integrals
      <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>\<assign\><sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*<space|0.17em>\<zeta\><around|(|K<rsub|z,f>|)>>
      satisfy <math|\<Psi\>*<around|(|v\<otimes\>f|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>v<around|(|z|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z>
      a.s. for <math|v\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>|)>>.

      <item>For all <math|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>> and
      <math|f,g\<in\>\<cal-S\><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
      <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z,g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>>.
      Hence <math|\<Psi\><around|(|z,\<cdot\>|)>> has an
      <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued version with
      law <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>, and the field
      <math|(\<Psi\>(z,\<cdot\><around|)||)><rsub|z\<in\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>>>
      is stationary and isotropic in <math|z>.
    </enumerate-alpha>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (a) <math|<math-ss|L>> is a continuous bijection of
    <math|\<cal-S\>*<around|(|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>\<times\>\<cal-M\>|)>>, so
    <math|\<Psi\><around|(|u|)>\<assign\><sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*\<zeta\><around|(|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1>u|)>>
    defines an <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'>>-valued variable with
    <math|\<Psi\><around|(|<math-ss|L>u|)>=<sqrt|4*\<pi\>>*\<zeta\><around|(|u|)>>.
    If <math|D\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'>> satisfies
    <math|D<around|(|<math-ss|L>u|)>=0> for all <math|u\<in\>\<cal-S\>>, then
    <math|D=0> since <math|<math-ss|L>> is onto: the solution is unique.

    (b), (c) Take the Fourier transform in <math|z> only, with measure
    <math|\<mathd\>q/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>. The transform of
    <math|K<rsub|z,f>> is the <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    function <math|q\<mapsto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<mathi\>q\<cdot\>z><around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-1>*f>.
    By Plancherel, the spectral theorem for <math|A> and Tonelli,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<langle\>|K<rsub|z,f>,K<rsub|z,g>|\<rangle\>>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>*g|\<rangle\>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>=<around*|\<langle\>|f,<around*|[|<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>|]>*g|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>>*<around|\<langle\>|f,A<rsup|-1>*g|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation*>

    using the scalar identity

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:4pi><big|int><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|2>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>*<frac|1|<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|r<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>r|<around|(|r<rsup|2>+a|)><rsup|2>>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>*a>,<space|2em>a\<gtr\>0.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|K<rsub|z,f>\<in\>L<rsup|2>>, and its continuity in
    <math|z> follows by dominated convergence. Since the transform of
    <math|<big|int>v<around|(|z|)>*K<rsub|z,f>\<mathd\>z> is
    <math|<wide|v|^><around|(|q|)>*<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-1>*f>,
    the Bochner integral <math|<big|int>v<around|(|z|)>*K<rsub|z,f>\<mathd\>z>
    equals <math|<math-ss|L><rsup|-1><around|(|v\<otimes\>f|)>>. The Wiener
    integral <math|\<zeta\>> is an isometry
    <math|L<rsup|2>\<to\>L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-P\>|)>> and commutes with
    Bochner integrals, which gives (b). Multiplying by <math|4*\<pi\>> gives
    the covariance in (c). The <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>-valued
    version is obtained as in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>.
    Stationarity and isotropy hold because
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|z<rprime|'>,g|)>|]>=4*\<pi\>*<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>q\<cdot\><around|(|z-z<rprime|'>|)>><around|\<langle\>|f,<around|(|<around|\||q|\|><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-2>*g|\<rangle\>><frac|\<mathd\>q|<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)><rsup|2>>>
    depends only on <math|<around|\||z-z<rprime|'>|\|>>.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Slices of Gaussian fields>Both stochastic quantisations exhibit
    the GFF as a <em|slice> of a Gaussian field in higher dimension. For the
    field on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> with covariance
    <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>+A|)><rsup|-p>>, the
    slice <math|<around|{|0|}>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> has covariance
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>*A<rsup|k/2-p>> with
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|p-<frac|k|2>|)>/<around|(|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|k/2>*\<Gamma\><around|(|p|)>|)>>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:slices>). The GFF arises iff <math|p=1+k/2>:
    <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|2,2|)>> is elliptic SQ, while
    <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>> gives instead the time-zero
    field <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>> of
    Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.4|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:RP> of Lecture 2. When
    <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, the elliptic field <math|\<Psi\>> is
    Euclidean invariant in <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d+2>>, and its restriction to
    <em|every> codimension-two affine subspace is a GFF.
  </remark>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Infinitesimal invariance characterises
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>>><label|ex:invariance>On
    <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>>, let <math|\<nu\>> be a probability
    measure on <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>|)>>
    with <math|\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>\<in\>L<rsup|1><around|(|\<nu\>|)>>
    and <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around|[|\<cal-L\>*F|]>=0> for all
    <math|F\<in\>\<cal-F\>*C<rsup|\<infty\>><rsub|b>>. Show that
    <math|\<nu\>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>. <em|Hint:> for
    <math|f=<big|sum><rsub|n\<leq\>N>a<rsub|n>*e<rsub|n>> and
    <math|\<psi\><around|(|a|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>\<varphi\><around|(|f|)>>>,
    show <math|<big|sum><rsub|n>\<lambda\><rsub|n>*a<rsub|n>*\<partial\><rsub|a<rsub|n>>*\<psi\>=-<around|\||a|\|><rsup|2>*\<psi\>>,
    and differentiate <math|s\<mapsto\>\<psi\><around|(|\<mathe\><rsup|s*D>a|)>*exp
    <around|(|<frac|1|2>*<big|sum><rsub|n>a<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<mathe\><rsup|2*\<lambda\><rsub|n>*s>/\<lambda\><rsub|n>|)>>,
    where <math|D=<math-up|diag><around|(|\<lambda\><rsub|n>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Reversibility>Use Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:inf-inv> to
    show that <math|\<cal-L\>> is symmetric in
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|m>|)>>. Deduce, and check directly
    from the covariance in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(a), that
    the stationary solution is time reversible:
    <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|-t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> and
    <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>> have the same
    law.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Mild implies weak><label|ex:weak> Prove the weak formulation in
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:parabolic>(b). Show that, as a random
    distribution on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>>, the stationary
    solution has covariance operator <math|2*<around|(|-\<partial\><rsub|t><rsup|2>+A<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Slices of Gaussian fields><label|ex:slices> Let <math|k\<geq\>1>,
    <math|p\<gtr\>k/2>, and let <math|\<Psi\>> be the centred Gaussian field
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> with covariance
    <math|<around|(|-\<Delta\><rsub|\<bbb-R\><rsup|k>>+A|)><rsup|-p>>. (a)
    Show that <math|\<Psi\><around|(|0,\<cdot\>|)>> is well defined with
    covariance <math|c<rsub|k,p>*A<rsup|k/2-p>>,
    <math|c<rsub|k,p>=\<Gamma\>*<around|(|p-<frac|k|2>|)>/<around|(|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|k/2>*\<Gamma\><around|(|p|)>|)>>.
    (b) For <math|<around|(|k,p|)>=<around|(|1,1|)>> and <math|\<cal-M\>=N>,
    show that the time-zero field of the GFF on <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>N> is
    the ground state measure <math|\<Omega\><rsub|0><around|(|\<varphi\>|)><rsup|2>*\<cal-D\>*\<varphi\>>
    of the free Hamiltonian, i.e. Gaussian with covariance
    <math|<around|(|2*\<omega\>|)><rsup|-1>> (compare
    Exercise<nbsp><hlink|2.2|cqft_notes_L1.tm#ex:OU>). (c) Show that the
    restriction of the elliptic field of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:elliptic>
    to a codimension-<em|one> slice <math|\<bbb-R\>\<times\>\<cal-M\>> has
    covariance proportional to <math|<around|(|-\<partial\><rsup|2>+A|)><rsup|-3/2>>,
    so it is not a GFF. (d) Show that <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|\<Psi\><around|(|z,f|)>*\<Psi\><around|(|0,g|)>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|f,<around|\||z|\|>*A<rsup|-1/2>*K<rsub|1>*<around|(|<around|\||z|\|>*A<rsup|1/2>|)>*g|\<rangle\>>>
    in Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:elliptic>, and recover
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,C*g|\<rangle\>>> as <math|z\<to\>0>.
  </exercise>

  <section|Setting: covariance profiles and observables><label|sec:obs>

  In Lectures 1\U2 a Euclidean field was a probability measure on
  <math|\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>, i.e. a random
  distribution <math|\<varphi\>>, and local operators were functions of
  <math|\<varphi\>>. This viewpoint hides the scale-by-scale organisation of
  the Wilson\UKadanoff picture<nbsp><cite|WK74>, and forces us to work with
  singular objects from the start. Already for the GFF, the Wick power
  <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>: is not a random distribution when
  <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d>, since its regularisations are not even
  tight (Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.11|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:no-wick> of
  Lecture 2).

  From now on we index everything by a <em|resolution>
  <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>. The field observed at resolution
  <math|t> is a smooth function <math|\<varphi\><rsub|t>>, and the
  information available at resolution <math|t> is the
  <math|\<sigma\>>-algebra <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> generated by
  <math|<around|(|\<varphi\><rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<leq\>t>>. A physical quantity
  is then not a single random variable but a <em|consistent family> of
  finite-resolution estimates of it, i.e. a martingale. This is the notion of
  stochastic observable of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>, on which this lecture is based;
  see also<nbsp><cite|Pol84|BK87|Sal99|BBD24> for the renormalisation group
  side and<nbsp><cite|BG20|BG24> for the variational side.

  In Section<nbsp><reference|sec:sq> the variable <math|t> was the auxiliary
  time of a stochastic dynamics; from now on <math|t> is a resolution.

  <subsection|Covariance profiles>

  <\definition>
    <label|def:profile>A <em|covariance profile> is a family
    <math|<around|(|Q<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>>> of
    bounded, symmetric, translation invariant operators on
    <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>> with <math|Q<rsub|0>=0>,
    <math|t\<mapsto\>Q<rsub|t>> smooth, <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<partial\><rsub|t>*Q<rsub|t>\<geq\>0>,
    <math|Q<rsub|t>\<to\>C> as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, and such that
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> and <math|Q<rsub|t>> have smooth kernels
    for every <math|t\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  </definition>

  Our standard example is the <em|heat profile>

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heat-profile>Q<rsub|t>\<assign\>C<space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>,<space|2em><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=<frac|2|t<rsup|3>><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>,
  </equation>

  which smooths the Green function with the heat kernel at time
  <math|t<rsup|-2>>. On <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>> the kernels are explicit,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:heat-kernel><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>=<frac|2*t<rsup|d-3>\<mathe\><rsup|-m<rsup|2>/t<rsup|2>>|<around|(|4*\<pi\>|)><rsup|d/2>><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-t<rsup|2>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|2>/4>,
  </equation>

  so <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> is concentrated on distances
  <math|\<lesssim\>1/t>, and in Fourier space <math|Q<rsub|t>> retains the
  modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<lesssim\>t>: <math|t> is a <em|momentum
  scale>, or resolution. Other profiles are useful, even when they violate
  some smoothness requirement of Definition<nbsp><reference|def:profile>. A
  sharp Fourier cutoff is <math|<wide|Q|^><rsub|t><around|(|k|)>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1><rsub|<around|\||k|\|>\<leq\>t>*<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
  which is not smooth in <math|t>. In a <em|finite range decomposition>,
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>=0> for
  <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<geq\>1/t> (Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:finite-range>).
  We write

  <\equation*>
    c<rsub|t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,x|)>,<space|2em>Q<rsub|T,t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>*<space|1em><around|(|t\<leq\>T|)>.
  </equation*>

  For <math|d\<geq\>2>, <math|c<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>> as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:ct>).

  Let <math|<around|(|W<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>> be a cylindrical Brownian
  motion on <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>,
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|[|W<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>*W<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>|]>=<around|(|t\<wedge\>s|)>*\<delta\>*<around|(|x-y|)>>,
  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:scale-GFF>X<rsub|t>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:scale-GFF>For every <math|t\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|X<rsub|t>> is a smooth Gaussian field with law
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>, and <math|<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>
    is a continuous martingale with <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|X<around|(|x|)>,X<around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>.
    As <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>, <math|X<rsub|t>\<to\>X<rsub|\<infty\>>> in
    <math|H<rsup|-s><rsub|<math-up|loc>>> for <math|s\<gtr\><frac|d|2>-1>,
    and <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|m>>.
    The increments <math|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>> are independent of
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<sigma\><around|(|X<rsub|s>:s\<leq\>t|)>>,
    with law <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>>>.
  </proposition>

  So the GFF is the terminal value of a Gaussian martingale of smooth fields:
  the <em|field at resolution <math|t>> (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:multiscale>).
  The proof is a variant of Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.2|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:existence>
  of Lecture 2 and is left as Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:scale-GFF>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|multiscale_gff.png>|1par|||><label|fig:multiscale>>|The
  field at resolution <math|t>, <math|X<rsub|t>>, for the heat profile on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>> (<math|m=4>, mean subtracted for display), at
  <math|t=8,32,128> and at the lattice cutoff, all built from the same white
  noise. The martingale <math|X<rsub|t>> reveals finer and finer details,
  while the large scales are fixed at low resolution.>

  <subsection|Stochastic observables>

  Fix a filtration <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>, for
  the moment that of <math|X>, and write <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\>[<space|0.17em>\<cdot\><space|0.17em><around|\||\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|]>>.

  <\definition>
    <dueto|<cite|BCG26>><label|def:obs> An <em|observable> is a real or
    complex <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)>>-martingale
    <math|O=<around|(|O<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>:
    <math|O<rsub|s>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|O<rsub|t>|]>> for
    <math|s\<leq\>t>. Its <em|expectation> is
    <math|<around|\<langle\>|O|\<rangle\>>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><around|[|O<rsub|t>|]>>,
    which is independent of <math|t>. An <em|observable field> is a family
    <math|<around|(|O<around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|x\<in\>\<cal-M\>>> of
    observables.
  </definition>

  Every integrable <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|\<infty\>>>-measurable random
  variable <math|Y> gives a <em|closed> observable
  <math|O<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|Y|]>>, with
  <math|O<rsub|t>\<to\>Y>. The point of the definition is that interesting
  observables need <em|not> be closed: a martingale may diverge as
  <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> (for instance <math|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> for
  <math|d\<geq\>2>), and still carry a perfectly meaningful family of
  finite-resolution estimates. The next lemma is elementary but basic.

  <\lemma>
    <dueto|Asymptotic uniqueness><label|lem:uniq> (a) If
    <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> are observables with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsup|1><rsub|t>-O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>,
    then <math|O<rsup|1>=O<rsup|2>>. (b) If <math|<around|(|N<rsub|t>|)>> is
    any adapted process and <math|O> an observable with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsub|t>-O<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>,
    then <math|O<rsub|s>=lim<rsub|t\<to\>\<infty\>>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|N<rsub|t>|]>> in <math|L<rsup|1>>.
  </lemma>

  <\proof>
    (a) <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|s>-O<rsup|2><rsub|s>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around|[|O<rsup|1><rsub|t>-O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|]>>
    for every <math|t\<geq\>s>, and conditional expectation is an
    <math|L<rsup|1>> contraction. (b) Likewise,
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|s>-\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*N<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<leq\><around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|t>-N<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>>.
  </proof>

  <\example>
    <dueto|Brownian observables><label|exa:brownian>Let <math|B> be a
    standard Brownian motion and <math|<around|(|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)>> its
    filtration. With <math|H<rsub|n><around|(|a;c|)>> the Hermite polynomials
    of variance <math|c>, the martingales

    <\equation*>
      B<rsub|t>,<space|2em>B<rsub|t><rsup|2>-t,<space|2em>B<rsub|t><rsup|3>-3*t*B<rsub|t>,<space|2em>\<ldots\>,<space|2em>H<rsub|n><around|(|B<rsub|t>;t|)>
    </equation*>

    are observables without a terminal value, and they are not even bounded
    in <math|L<rsup|1>>: <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\||B<rsub|t><rsup|2>-t|\|>=t*\<bbb-E\><around|\||Z<rsup|2>-1|\|>\<to\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|Z\<sim\>N<around|(|0,1|)>>. They are the prototypes of the Wick
    powers of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:gff-obs>: at a fixed point,
    <math|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> is a continuous Gaussian martingale with
    quadratic variation <math|c<rsub|t>>, i.e. a Brownian motion run with the
    clock <math|c<rsub|t>>. The exponential martingale

    <\equation*>
      Z<rsub|t>=exp <around*|(|\<lambda\>*B<rsub|t>-<frac|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|2>*t|)>=<big|sum><rsub|n\<geq\>0><frac|\<lambda\><rsup|n>|n!>*H<rsub|n><around|(|B<rsub|t>;t|)>,<space|2em>\<lambda\>\<neq\>0,
    </equation*>

    makes the loss of terminal information explicit: <math|Z<rsub|t>\<to\>0>
    almost surely, while <math|\<bbb-E\>*Z<rsub|t>=1> for every <math|t>. The
    almost sure limit is zero, the observable is not: the mass escapes to
    rarer and rarer paths, and the limit forgets all the finite-resolution
    expectations.
  </example>

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Repair by the future drift><label|prop:repair>Let
    <math|N<rsub|t>=N<rsub|0>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>\<beta\><rsub|s>*\<mathd\>s+M<rsub|t>>,
    with <math|N<rsub|0>\<in\>L<rsup|1>>, <math|M> a martingale and
    <math|\<bbb-E\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|s>|\|>*\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Then

    <\equation*>
      O<rsub|t>\<assign\>N<rsub|t>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>\<beta\><rsub|s>*\<mathd\>s|]>
    </equation*>

    is an observable, and it is the only observable with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|N<rsub|t>-O<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <math|O<rsub|t>=N<rsub|0>+M<rsub|t>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>>\<beta\><rsub|s>*\<mathd\>s|]>>
    is a sum of martingales, and <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|t>-N<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<leq\>\<bbb-E\><big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<beta\><rsub|s>|\|>*\<mathd\>s\<to\>0>.
    Uniqueness is Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:uniq>(a).
  </proof>

  The correction is the expected drift still to come. For instance, for
  <math|N<rsub|t>=B<rsub|t>+\<mathe\><rsup|-t>*sin B<rsub|t>> Itô's formula
  gives <math|\<beta\><rsub|t>=-<frac|3|2>*\<mathe\><rsup|-t>*sin B<rsub|t>>,
  and since <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*sin B<rsub|s>=\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|s-t|)>/2>*sin
  B<rsub|t>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>\<beta\><rsub|s>*\<mathd\>s|]>=-<frac|3|2>*sin
    B<rsub|t>*<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|-s-<around|(|s-t|)>/2>*\<mathd\>s=-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>*sin
    B<rsub|t>,
  </equation*>

  so the observable in the class of <math|N> is <math|O<rsub|t>=B<rsub|t>>.
  This correction is the germ of the forward\Ubackward equations of Lecture
  4: there the forecast is obtained from the field in the same way, by adding
  the expected drift still to come.

  <\example>
    <dueto|A delta at a Brownian endpoint><label|exa:delta>Fix
    <math|T\<gtr\>0>, <math|y\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, let
    <math|p<rsub|s><around|(|z|)>=<around|(|2*\<pi\>*s|)><rsup|-1/2>*\<mathe\><rsup|-z<rsup|2>/2*s>>
    be the heat kernel, and

    <\equation*>
      \<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>\<assign\>p<rsub|T-t><around|(|B<rsub|t>-y|)>,<space|2em>0\<leq\>t\<less\>T.
    </equation*>

    Here the resolution runs over <math|<around|[|0,T|)>> (reparametrise by
    <math|<around|(|T-t|)><rsup|-1>> to get
    <math|<around|[|T<rsup|-1>,\<infty\>|)>>). The heat semigroup gives
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>=\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|s>>,
    and <math|<big|int>g<around|(|y|)>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>*\<mathd\>y=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*g<around|(|B<rsub|T>|)>>
    for bounded <math|g>. At fixed <math|y>,
    <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>\<to\>0> almost surely as
    <math|t\<uparrow\>T>, but <math|\<bbb-E\>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>=p<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>\<gtr\>0>.
    So <math|\<delta\><around|(|B<rsub|T>-y|)>> is not a random variable, but
    it is an observable: the family of its conditional densities. The same
    construction works for any Markov process with a transition density.
  </example>

  So an observable is determined by its asymptotic behaviour. A <em|germ> for
  <math|O> is a family of functionals <math|<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t>>
  on smooth fields such that <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|O<rsub|t>-<wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>.
  The observable field <math|O<around|(|x|)>> is <em|local> if it admits
  germs of the form

  <\equation*>
    <wide|O|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)><around|(|x|)>=<wide|\<cal-O\>|\<abovering\>><rsub|t><around*|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,\<nabla\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,\<ldots\>,\<nabla\><rsup|k>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Germs are not unique, and this freedom reflects the freedom of choosing
  counterterms. By Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:uniq>(b), a germ determines the
  observable.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The field at resolution <math|t>><label|ex:scale-GFF> (a) Prove
    Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:scale-GFF>. <em|Hint:> on the torus,
    write <math|X<rsub|t>=<big|sum><rsub|n><around|(|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|q|\<dot\>><rsub|n><around|(|s|)><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>B<rsup|n><rsub|s>|)>*e<rsub|n>>.
    (b) For the heat profile, check <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s=C>
    and <math|Q<rsub|t>=C\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>>, and derive
    <eqref|eq:heat-kernel>. (c) Show that
    <math|x\<mapsto\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>> is a.s. real analytic for the
    heat profile.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Divergence of <math|c<rsub|t>>><label|ex:ct> For the heat profile
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>, show that
    <math|c<rsub|t>=<frac|1|4*\<pi\>>*E<rsub|1>*<around|(|m<rsup|2>/t<rsup|2>|)>=<frac|1|2*\<pi\>>*log
    <frac|t|m>+O<around|(|1|)>> for <math|d=2>, where <math|E<rsub|1>> is the
    exponential integral, and that <math|c<rsub|t>\<asymp\>t<rsup|d-2>> for
    <math|d\<geq\>3>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Brownian observables><label|ex:brownian-obs>(a) Show that
    <math|H<rsub|n><around|(|B<rsub|t>;t|)>> is a martingale and that
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\||H<rsub|n><around|(|B<rsub|t>;t|)>|\|>=t<rsup|n/2>*\<bbb-E\><around|\||H<rsub|n><around|(|Z;1|)>|\|>>.
    (b) Show that the exponential martingale of
    Example<nbsp><reference|exa:brownian> converges to <math|0> almost surely
    and is not uniformly integrable. Which observables of Brownian motion are
    closed? (c) Check the computation of the repaired observable for
    <math|N<rsub|t>=B<rsub|t>+\<mathe\><rsup|-t>*sin B<rsub|t>>, and find the
    observable in the class of <math|N<rsub|t>=B<rsub|t>+\<mathe\><rsup|-t>*B<rsub|t><rsup|2>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Brownian deltas><label|ex:brownian-delta>(a) Prove the properties
    of <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y>> stated in Example<nbsp><reference|exa:delta>.
    (b) Show that <math|y\<mapsto\>\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>> is a probability
    density for every <math|t\<less\>T>: the family
    <math|<around|(|\<Pi\><rsup|y>|)><rsub|y>> is a measure-valued observable
    of total mass one, although each <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>> tends to
    zero. (c) Show that <math|\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|k>*\<Pi\><rsup|y>> is
    an observable, with <math|<big|int>g<around|(|y|)>*\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|k>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>*\<mathd\>y=<around|(|-1|)><rsup|k>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*g<rsup|<around|(|k|)>><around|(|B<rsub|T>|)>>
    for smooth bounded <math|g>: the observable of
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|k>*\<delta\><around|(|B<rsub|T>-y|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <section|Observables of the Gaussian free field and their
  products><label|sec:gff-obs>

  <subsection|Wick powers and exponentials>

  <paragraph|Point evaluations.> For every <math|x> and every <math|d>,
  <math|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>\<assign\><around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>|)><rsub|t\<geq\>0>>
  is a local observable. For <math|d\<geq\>2> it is not closed:
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=c<rsub|t>\<to\>\<infty\>>.

  <paragraph|Wick powers.> Recall that <math|\<partial\><rsub|c>*H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>=-<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|2>*H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>>
  and <math|\<partial\><rsub|a>*H<rsub|n>=n*H<rsub|n-1>> (differentiate the
  generating function <math|\<mathe\><rsup|\<tau\>*a-c*\<tau\><rsup|2>/2>>).

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Wick observables><label|prop:wick-obs> For every <math|d\<geq\>1>,
    <math|n\<geq\>1> and <math|x\<in\>\<cal-M\>>,

    <\equation*>
      :X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>
    </equation*>

    is a continuous martingale, with <math|\<mathd\><space|0.17em>:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>=n<space|0.17em>:X<rsup|n-1><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>
    and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:wick-obs-cov>\<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)><space|0.17em>:X<rsup|k><rsub|t>:<around|(|y|)>|]>=\<delta\><rsub|n*k>*<space|0.17em>n!<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)><rsup|n>.
    </equation>

    In particular, for <math|x\<neq\>y>, <math|lim<rsub|t>
    \<bbb-E\>[:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>:X<rsup|n><rsub|t>:<around|(|y|)>]=n!<space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By Itô's formula, using <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|X<around|(|x|)>,X<around|(|x|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<wide|c|\<dot\>><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t>,

    <\equation*>
      \<mathd\>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>=\<partial\><rsub|a>*H<rsub|n><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>+<around*|(|<tfrac|1|2>\<partial\><rsub|a><rsup|2>*H<rsub|n>+\<partial\><rsub|c>*H<rsub|n>|)>*<wide|c|\<dot\>><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t=n*H<rsub|n-1>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>.
    </equation*>

    The covariance is Lemma<nbsp><hlink|3.8|cqft_notes_L2.tm#lem:hermite> of
    Lecture 2, applied to the Gaussian vector
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>|)>>.
  </proof>

  Compare with Theorem<nbsp><hlink|3.9|cqft_notes_L2.tm#thm:wick> of Lecture
  2: there <math|:\<varphi\><rsup|n>>: existed as a random distribution only
  for <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<less\>d>, and by
  Proposition<nbsp><hlink|3.11|cqft_notes_L2.tm#prop:no-wick> not at all
  otherwise. As observables, Wick powers exist in <em|every> dimension and
  need no smearing. When <math|n*<around|(|d-2|)>\<geq\>d> and
  <math|0\<leq\>f\<nequiv\>0>, the smeared martingale
  <math|t\<mapsto\>:X<rsub|t><rsup|n>:<around|(|f|)>> is unbounded in
  <math|L<rsup|2>> and, by the Paley\UZygmund argument of Lecture 2, not even
  tight as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>: it has no terminal value, and the
  observable is the whole family, not a limit. The singularity has not
  disappeared. It has moved from the observables themselves to their
  <em|products> at coincident points, as we now see.

  <paragraph|Exponentials.> For <math|\<beta\>\<in\>\<bbb-C\>>,
  <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|x|)>\<assign\>exp
  <around|(|\<beta\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>-<frac|\<beta\><rsup|2>|2>*c<rsub|t>|)>>
  is the stochastic exponential of <math|\<beta\>*X<around|(|x|)>>, hence a
  martingale, and <math|\<bbb-E\>[:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|1>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|x|)>:\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|2>*X<rsub|t>>:<around|(|y|)>]=\<mathe\><rsup|\<beta\><rsub|1>*\<beta\><rsub|2>*Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>>>.

  <subsection|Products of observables><label|sec:product>

  The pointwise product of two martingales is not a martingale. The natural
  definition of the product keeps the asymptotics.

  <\definition>
    <label|def:product>Two observables <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> <em|admit a
    product> if <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>\<in\>L<rsup|1>> for
    all <math|t> and there is an observable <math|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>> with <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|t>-O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>\<to\>0>. By
    Lemma<nbsp><reference|lem:uniq> it is unique, and
    <math|<around|(|O<rsup|1> \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|s>=lim<rsub|t>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|]>>.
  </definition>

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:product>Let <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> be continuous
    observables, square integrable on bounded intervals, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:prod-cond>\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|s>|\|>\<less\>\<infty\>.
    </equation>

    Then <math|O<rsup|1> \<star\>*O<rsup|2>> exists and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:prod-formula><around|(|O<rsup|1>
      \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|)><rsub|t>=O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
      O<rsup|2><rsub|t>+\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|s>|]>.
    </equation>

    In particular <math|<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>
    \<star\>*O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>=O<rsup|1><rsub|0>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|0>+\<bbb-E\>*<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    <math|P<rsub|t>\<assign\>O<rsup|1><rsub|t>
    O<rsup|2><rsub|t>-<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>>
    is a martingale on bounded intervals, and
    <math|N<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>
    is a closed martingale by <eqref|eq:prod-cond>. So <math|P+N> is a
    martingale and equals the right-hand side of <eqref|eq:prod-formula>. Its
    distance from <math|O<rsup|1><rsub|t> O<rsup|2><rsub|t>> in
    <math|L<rsup|1>> is at most <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>>|\|>\<to\>0>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Brownian deltas.> The observables <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y>> of
  Example<nbsp><reference|exa:delta> show the mechanism of the operator
  product expansion in its simplest form. Write
  <math|c<rsub|s>=<around|(|4*\<pi\>*s|)><rsup|-1/2>>. Completing the square,
  <math|<around|(|B<rsub|t>-y|)><rsup|2>+<around|(|B<rsub|t>-z|)><rsup|2>=2*<around|(|B<rsub|t>-m|)><rsup|2>+<around|(|y-z|)><rsup|2>/2>
  with <math|m=<around|(|y+z|)>/2>, gives the exact identity

  <\equation*>
    \<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>*\<Pi\><rsup|z><rsub|t>=c<rsub|T-t>*\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|y-z|)><rsup|2>/4*<around|(|T-t|)>>*p<rsub|<around|(|T-t|)>/2><around|(|B<rsub|t>-m|)>,<space|2em>t\<less\>T.
  </equation*>

  Since <math|B<rsub|t>\<sim\>N<around|(|0,t|)>>,
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*p<rsub|<around|(|T-t|)>/2><around|(|B<rsub|t>-m|)>=p<rsub|<around|(|T+t|)>/2><around|(|m|)>>,
  so for <math|y\<neq\>z>

  <\equation*>
    <around|\<\|\|\>|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>*\<Pi\><rsup|z><rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|1>>=c<rsub|T-t>*\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|y-z|)><rsup|2>/4*<around|(|T-t|)>>*p<rsub|<around|(|T+t|)>/2><around|(|m|)>\<longrightarrow\>0<space|2em><around|(|t\<uparrow\>T|)>:
  </equation*>

  the product exists and <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y>\<star\>\<Pi\><rsup|z>=0>. At
  <math|y=z> the exponential suppression is lost, and
  <math|\<bbb-E\>*<around|(|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|t>|)><rsup|2>=c<rsub|T-t>*p<rsub|<around|(|T+t|)>/2><around|(|y|)>\<to\>\<infty\>>:
  the square does not exist. It can be resolved at a fixed coarser
  resolution. Let <math|u=T-\<varepsilon\>> be a fine resolution and
  <math|s\<less\>u> a coarser one. Since <math|p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|w|)><rsup|2>=c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>/2><around|(|w|)>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around*|[|<around|(|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|T-\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|2>|]>=c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*p<rsub|T-s-\<varepsilon\>/2><around|(|B<rsub|s>-y|)>=c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|s>-<frac|\<varepsilon\>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|4>*\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|s>+<frac|\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|32>*\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|4>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|s>+O<rsub|L<rsup|1>><around|(|\<varepsilon\><rsup|5/2>|)>,
  </equation*>

  by Taylor expansion in the time variable and
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|s>*p<rsub|s>=<frac|1|2>*\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|2>*p<rsub|s>>.
  This is an operator product expansion: singular coefficients
  (<math|c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>\<infty\>>, while
  <math|\<varepsilon\>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>,\<varepsilon\><rsup|2>*c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>0>)
  times the observables <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y>>,
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|y><rsup|2>*\<Pi\><rsup|y>>, <math|\<ldots\>>. The
  order of the limits matters: the remainder is small only after conditioning
  at a fixed resolution <math|s>. Without conditioning,
  <math|<around|(|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|T-\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|2>-c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|T-\<varepsilon\>>=c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*<around|[|p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>/2>-p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>|]><around|(|B<rsub|T-\<varepsilon\>>-y|)>>
  has <math|L<rsup|1>> norm of order <math|\<varepsilon\><rsup|-1/2>>.

  <paragraph|Products of point evaluations.> Here
  <math|<around|\<langle\>|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>,<math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
  is deterministic and <eqref|eq:prod-cond> reads
  <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\||<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>|\|>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
  For the heat profile and <math|d\<geq\>2> this holds iff <math|x\<neq\>y>,
  and then <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>\<geq\>0> and the integral is
  <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>>. Hence

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:XX><around|(|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|t>=X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>*X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>+G*<around|(|x-y|)>-Q<rsub|t>*<around|(|x-y|)>,<space|2em><around|\<langle\>|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|\<rangle\>>=G*<around|(|x-y|)>,
  </equation>

  which is the correct two-point function. We never had to discuss the
  regularity of <math|X<rsub|\<infty\>>>. For <math|x=y> and <math|d\<geq\>2>
  the product does not exist: its expectation would be <math|lim<rsub|t>
  \<bbb-E\>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>=lim<rsub|t> c<rsub|t>=\<infty\>>.

  <paragraph|Operator product expansion.> Formula <eqref|eq:XX> has an exact
  short-distance expansion at every resolution: for fixed <math|t>, as
  <math|y\<to\>x>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:OPE><around|(|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>|)><rsub|t>-G*<around|(|x-y|)><space|0.27em>\<longrightarrow\><space|0.27em>X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>-c<rsub|t>=:X<rsup|2><rsub|t>:<around|(|x|)>.
  </equation>

  Since both sides are martingales, this reads
  <math|<math-bf|X><around|(|x|)>*\<star\><math-bf|X><around|(|y|)>=G*<around|(|x-y|)>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+:<math-bf|X><rsup|2>:<around|(|x|)>+o<around|(|1|)>>,
  the remainder tending to zero at each fixed resolution <math|t>: the Wick
  square is the regular part of the product, and <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>> is
  its singular coefficient. More generally
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:wick-product>), for <math|x\<neq\>y>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:wick-product>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|k>:<around|(|y|)>=<big|sum><rsub|j\<leq\>n\<wedge\>k>j!<binom|n|j><binom|k|j><space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|j><space|0.17em>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n-j><around|(|x|)><math-bf|X><rsup|k-j><around|(|y|)>:,
  </equation>

  where <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|p><around|(|x|)><math-bf|X><rsup|q><around|(|y|)>:<rsub|t>\<assign\>H<rsub|p>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>*H<rsub|q>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|y|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>>
  minus its contractions at scales <math|\<leq\>t>. This is Wick's theorem
  written at the level of observables. The coefficients
  <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|j>> carry all the singularities, and the
  observables on the right have regular limits as <math|y\<to\>x>.

  <paragraph|Higher orders.> The expansion <eqref|eq:OPE> continues at every
  fixed resolution. With <math|x<rsub|\<pm\>>=x\<pm\>\<rho\>*e/2>,
  <math|<around|\||e|\|>=1>, <math|\<partial\>=e\<cdot\>\<nabla\>> and
  <math|W<rsub|t><around|(|U,V|)>\<assign\>U<rsub|t>*V<rsub|t>-\<bbb-E\><around|[|U<rsub|t>*V<rsub|t>|]>>,

  <\equation*>
    <around|(|X<around|(|x<rsub|->|)>\<star\>X<around|(|x<rsub|+>|)>|)><rsub|t>=G<around|(|\<rho\>*e|)>+:X<rsub|t><rsup|2>:<around|(|x|)>+\<rho\><rsup|2>*C<rsub|2,t><around|(|x|)>+\<rho\><rsup|4>*C<rsub|4,t><around|(|x|)>+O<rsub|L<rsup|1>><around|(|\<rho\><rsup|6>|)>,
  </equation*>

  <\equation*>
    C<rsub|2>=<frac|1|4>*<around*|(|W<around|(|X,\<partial\><rsup|2>*X|)>-W<around|(|\<partial\>*X,\<partial\>*X|)>|)>,<space|2em>C<rsub|4>=<frac|1|192>*<around*|(|W<around|(|X,\<partial\><rsup|4>*X|)>-4*W<around|(|\<partial\>*X,\<partial\><rsup|3>*X|)>+3*W<around|(|\<partial\><rsup|2>*X,\<partial\><rsup|2>*X|)>|)>.
  </equation*>

  Subtracting <math|G> leaves the bilocal Wick product
  <math|X<rsub|t><around|(|x<rsub|->|)>*X<rsub|t><around|(|x<rsub|+>|)>-Q<rsub|t><around|(|x<rsub|->,x<rsub|+>|)>>,
  which is smooth in <math|\<rho\>> at fixed <math|t>, and the symmetry
  <math|\<rho\>\<mapsto\>-\<rho\>> kills the odd orders. Each coefficient is
  a finite sum of Wick bilinears, hence an observable. The constant in the
  remainder depends on <math|t>; the limit <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>> is a
  separate question. A systematic study of the operator product expansion in
  this framework is due to P.<nbsp>Paulovics (in preparation).

  <paragraph|Locality and factorisation.> Let <math|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>> be
  observables with germs supported in <math|U<rsup|1><rsub|1/t>> and
  <math|U<rsup|2><rsub|1/t>>, where <math|U<rsup|i>> are sets at positive
  distance and <math|U<rsub|r>> is the <math|r>-neighbourhood of <math|U>.
  The covariation <math|\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|O<rsup|1>,O<rsup|2>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=<around|\<langle\>|D*O<rsup|1><rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>D*O<rsup|2><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>t>
  involves <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> only between the two supports. For
  a finite range decomposition it vanishes for large <math|t>, so the product
  exists and is asymptotically the pointwise product. For the heat profile it
  is exponentially small, by <eqref|eq:heat-kernel>. Thus, under
  integrability conditions on the drifts of the germs, observables supported
  on disjoint regions can be multiplied, while the products of observables at
  the same point require renormalisation (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:locality>).
  This is the structure of a <em|factorisation algebra> of observables; the
  precise axioms are not established here<nbsp><cite|BCG26>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|locality.png>|1par|||><label|fig:locality>>|Covariation
  of point observables, heat profile in <math|d=2>. Left: the kernels of
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>> centred at <math|x> (navy) and at
  <math|y> (teal) at three resolutions, with their overlap (gold). The
  integral of the product of the two kernels is the covariation density
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>. Right:
  <math|s\<mapsto\><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>> for three
  distances (<math|m=1>). Its integral is <math|G*<around|(|x-y|)>>, and it
  is negligible for <math|s\<gg\>1/<around|\||x-y|\|>>. As <math|y\<to\>x>
  the mass moves to <math|s\<to\>\<infty\>>, which is the divergence of the
  product at coincident points.>

  <\remark>
    <label|rem:finite-range>A finite range decomposition makes these
    statements exact: if <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|x,y|)>=0>
    for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<geq\>1/t>, then <math|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>>
    restricted to regions at distance <math|\<geq\>1/t> are independent. Such
    decompositions of <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> exist
    and are a basic tool of the rigorous renormalisation
    group<nbsp><cite|BBS19>.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|Outlook.> Interactions are introduced in Lecture 4. The
  effective potentials solve the Polchinski equation, and the interacting
  field seen at increasing resolution solves a forward\Ubackward stochastic
  differential equation whose force is itself an observable. Products, the
  operator product expansion and the locality of observables, which we met
  here for the GFF, are the tools to describe it.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Hermite calculus along the flow><label|ex:hermite-flow> (a) Show
    that <math|<big|int>H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a+b;c|)>*<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|s><around|(|\<mathd\>b|)>=H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c-s|)>>
    for <math|0\<leq\>s\<leq\>c>, where <math|\<mu\><rsup|s>=N<around|(|0,s|)>>.
    (b) Deduce again that <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|X<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|t>|)>>
    is a martingale, using only the independence of the increments.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Products of Wick observables><label|ex:wick-product> (a) Show that
    <eqref|eq:prod-cond> holds for <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>>
    and <math|:<math-bf|X><rsup|k>:<around|(|y|)>> when <math|x\<neq\>y> and
    <math|Q> is the heat profile. <em|Hint:> <math|c<rsub|s>> grows at most
    polynomially while <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
    decays like <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-s<rsup|2>*<around|\||x-y|\|><rsup|2>/4>>.
    (b) Show that <math|\<langle\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\>:<math-bf|X><rsup|n>:<around|(|y|)>\<rangle\>=n<rsup|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|\<infty\>><around|(|n-1|)>!<space|0.17em>Q<rsub|s><rsup|n-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s=n!<space|0.17em>G*<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|n>>.
    (c) Prove <eqref|eq:wick-product>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Exponential observables>For <math|\<beta\>=\<mathi\>b>,
    <math|b\<in\>\<bbb-R\>>, and the heat profile, show that condition
    <eqref|eq:prod-cond> for <math|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>b<math-bf|X>>:<around|(|x|)>*\<star\><wide|:\<mathe\><rsup|\<mathi\>b<math-bf|X>>:<around|(|y|)>|\<bar\>>>
    reads <math|<big|int><rsup|\<infty\>>\<mathe\><rsup|b<rsup|2>*c<rsub|s>><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    Deduce that the product exists for all <math|x\<neq\>y> if
    <math|d\<leq\>3>, for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<gtr\><around|\||b|\|>/2*\<pi\>>
    but not for <math|<around|\||x-y|\|>\<less\><around|\||b|\|>/2*\<pi\>> if
    <math|d=4>, and not at all if <math|d\<geq\>5>. For <math|d\<leq\>3> show
    that its expectation is <math|\<mathe\><rsup|b<rsup|2>*G*<around|(|x-y|)>>>,
    and decide for which <math|b> it is locally integrable in <math|y>: all
    <math|b> in <math|d=1>, <math|b<rsup|2>\<less\>4*\<pi\>> in <math|d=2>
    (the <math|L<rsup|2>> threshold of Lecture 2), only <math|b=0> in
    <math|d=3>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The Brownian OPE><label|ex:brownian-ope>(a) Prove the product
    identity for <math|p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|B-y|)>*p<rsub|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|B-z|)>>
    and deduce <math|\<Pi\><rsup|y>\<star\>\<Pi\><rsup|z>=0> for
    <math|y\<neq\>z>. (b) Derive the expansion of
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|s><around|[|<around|(|\<Pi\><rsup|y><rsub|T-\<varepsilon\>>|)><rsup|2>|]>>
    to all orders in <math|\<varepsilon\>>, and bound the remainder in
    <math|L<rsup|1>> at fixed <math|s>. (c) Show that the unconditioned
    remainder has <math|L<rsup|1>> norm <math|\<sim\>c<rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*p<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>*<big|int><around|\||p<rsub|1/2>-p<rsub|1>|\|>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Point splitting><label|ex:point-splitting>(a) Derive the
    coefficients <math|C<rsub|2>> and <math|C<rsub|4>> above by Taylor
    expanding <math|X<rsub|t><around|(|x\<pm\>\<rho\>*e/2|)>>. (b) Show that
    <math|\<bbb-E\>*C<rsub|2,t>=0> and compute
    <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|C<rsub|2,t><around|(|x|)><rsup|2>|]>> for the
    heat profile. How does it grow as <math|t\<to\>\<infty\>>?
  </exercise>

