    <\associate|figure>
      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|4.1>||Parabolic
      stochastic quantisation of the GFF on a circle of length
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|8>, <with|mode|<quote|math>|m=1>, started from
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<Phi\><rsub|0>=0>. Left: a sample of the
      solution. Right: each Fourier mode is an Ornstein\UUhlenbeck process,
      whose variance relaxes to <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<lambda\><rsub|k><rsup|-1>>
      at rate <with|mode|<quote|math>|2*\<lambda\><rsub|k>> (Theorem
      <no-break><specific|screen|<resize|<move|<with|color|<quote|#A0A0FF>|->|-0.3em|>|0em||0em|>><reference|thm:parabolic>(c)).
      Dots: Monte Carlo estimates with <with|mode|<quote|math>|4000>
      samples.>|<pageref|auto-8>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|5.1>||The field at
      resolution <with|mode|<quote|math>|t>,
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|X<rsub|t>>, for the heat profile on
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>
      (<with|mode|<quote|math>|m=4>, mean subtracted for display), at
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|t=8,32,128> and at the lattice cutoff, all
      built from the same white noise. The martingale
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|X<rsub|t>> reveals finer and finer details,
      while the large scales are fixed at low resolution.>|<pageref|auto-13>>

      <tuple|normal|<surround|<hidden-binding|<tuple>|6.1>||Covariation of
      point observables, heat profile in <with|mode|<quote|math>|d=2>. Left:
      the kernels of <with|mode|<quote|math>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>>
      centred at <with|mode|<quote|math>|x> (navy) and at
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|y> (teal) at three resolutions, with their
      overlap (gold). The integral of the product of the two kernels is the
      covariation density <with|mode|<quote|math>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>.
      Right: <with|mode|<quote|math>|s\<mapsto\><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*<around|(|x-y|)>>
      for three distances (<with|mode|<quote|math>|m=1>). Its integral is
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|G*<around|(|x-y|)>>, and it is negligible for
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|s\<gg\>1/<around|\||x-y|\|>>. As
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|y\<to\>x> the mass moves to
      <with|mode|<quote|math>|s\<to\>\<infty\>>, which is the divergence of
      the product at coincident points.>|<pageref|auto-27>>
    </associate>
